xnu 0141: the real read framing (0xFF dummy bytes) and the driver's power order

Lineage's open driver for the same hardware is in the public kernel tree
(drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix_berlin_driver). It settles two things:

- A "9916" is BerlinD there, and BerlinD uses goodix_spi_read, whose three
  dummy bytes are 0xFF, not the zeros this driver sent. Every read so far had
  a frame the part never saw as a read.
- brl_power_on enables IOVDD, waits 3 ms, drives AVDD, then holds reset low
  for 15 ms, releases it high and waits before brl_dev_confirm (eight 0xAA
  bytes at 0x10000, read back and compared).

Follow both: 0xFF dummies and the source's order, and make the probe the
device-confirm transfer itself instead of a matrix of guesses.

